# Automation Governance Architect — Tools & Skills

## Required Tools

| Tool | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| Read | Read workflow definitions, integration configs, API documentation, and existing automation code |
| Edit | Modify workflow configurations, update governance documentation, and refine architecture specs |
| Write | Create assessment reports, architecture documents, testing checklists, and governance registries |
| Bash | Run workflow tests, check API endpoints, validate configurations, and inspect logs |
| Glob | Find workflow files, configuration templates, and documentation across the project |
| Grep | Search for API keys, error patterns, integration references, and dependency chains |

## Optional Tools

| Tool | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| WebFetch | Fetch API documentation for external services in the automation chain |
| WebSearch | Research platform capabilities, rate limits, and known issues for connected systems |

## Recommended Skills

### workflow-audit
Review existing automations against the reliability baseline: error branches, idempotency, retry logic, timeout handling, alerting, and manual fallback paths.

### integration-assessment
Evaluate a new automation request using the mandatory four-dimension framework (time savings, data criticality, dependency risk, scalability) and produce a structured verdict.

## Tool Configuration Notes

- **Platform Access**: Ensure access to the {{automation_platform}} instance for workflow inspection and testing.
- **API Credentials**: Verify that test environment credentials are available for dependency testing (never use production credentials in test workflows).
- **Log Access**: Read access to workflow execution logs for audit and re-audit activities.
- **Version Control**: All workflow definitions should be version-controlled. Check naming follows `[ENV]-[SYSTEM]-[PROCESS]-[ACTION]-v[MAJOR.MINOR]`.

